  "question": "Does MORE evidence make the director better? The previous sweep compared medians of three repeats and found +0.1111, which is exactly one quantum of a metric whose run-to-run spread is one to two quanta. This file asks the question in the unit the metric can resolve: claims, in pairs.",
  "pre_registered_rules": {
    "more_evidence_helps_if": "a paired CLAIM-level test (exact McNemar on the nine false claims, each claim aggregated across the repeats) rejects at p < 0.05 with more claims caught at the larger budget",
    "the_unit_of_analysis_is": "THE CLAIM (nine of them), not the claim-repeat. The same nine claims are measured at every repeat, so pooling the repeats gives twenty-seven observations of nine things treated as independent. That is anti-conservative, and it is not a small correction: on the 60,000 -> 140,000 step the pooled p is 0.031 and the claim-level p is 0.500.",
    "the_effect_is_below_the_noise_if": "the paired test does not reject at p < 0.05, OR the design cannot reach p < 0.05 at any effect size",
    "the_claim_level_test_is_valid_if": "the NULL ARM -- a budget compared with itself -- gives zero discordant claims and p = 1.0",
    "the_test_can_come_out_the_other_way_if": "the REVERSED pairing reports the opposite direction or an opposite sign",
    "verdict_is_written_before_the_numbers_are_read": true
  },
